invariant: INV-16
file: mlkem_poly.c
catches: test/lint-wide-multiply.sh
reason: mlk_poly_frommsg builds each coefficient's mask from the message
  bit without reading the bit back from a volatile object. The compiler
  then knows the bit is 0 or 1, and clang for x86-64 writes the mask as a
  choice between 1665 and 0 with a jump on the bit, which is a bit of the
  message a shared secret is derived from. The coefficients are the same,
  so the vectors and the proofs pass. On the x86-64 spec mlkem_poly.c@host
  goes to 69 against its ceiling of 59 and mlkem_poly_native.c to 71
  against 61: eight jumps on the bits of a byte, in the loop clang keeps
  for overlapping arguments, and two branches of the vector loop beside
  it. The arm64 spec's three copies each go two over, for the vector loop
  alone (docs/decisions.md 125 and 126).

--- old
            opaque = ((uint32_t)msg[i] >> j) & 1U;
            uint32_t bit = opaque;
--- new
            uint32_t bit = ((uint32_t)msg[i] >> j) & 1U;
